<script>
import DealActions from 'crm/src/deals/lib/DealActions.abide'
import dealActionsSource from 'crm/src/deals/lib/DealActions.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import dealActionsTestSource from 'crm/src/deals/lib/DealActions.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import StageHints from 'crm/src/pipeline/lib/StageHints.abide'
import stageHintsSource from 'crm/src/pipeline/lib/StageHints.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import stageHintsTestSource from 'crm/src/pipeline/lib/StageHints.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }

import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Registry from '#src/lib/Registry.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import Snippet from '#src/lib/Snippet.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="A hint on a pipeline stage, on hover and on focus">
        <Example
            id="tooltip-stage-hints"
            title={'`<Tooltip>` — a hint for the element before it'}
            summary="A new rep reads what each pipeline stage means by resting the pointer on it, or by tabbing to it."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/StageHints.abide': stageHintsSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/StageHints.test.ts': stageHintsTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <StageHints />
        </Example>

        <p>Write <code>Tooltip</code> right after the element it explains, with the hint as its
            children. That element is its anchor, and its <code>aria-describedby</code> points at
            the hint. The hint opens after the pointer rests there for <code>delay</code>
            milliseconds, 300 by default, so passing over a row of stages opens nothing. Focus
            opens it at once. Leaving, blurring or Escape closes it.</p>

        <p>The hint is a <code>popover="manual"</code> element, so it opens in the top layer,
            above every stacking context and clipped by no scroller. It opens on
            <code>side</code>, top by default, and flips to the opposite side where there is no
            room. Give the anchor a <code>tabindex</code> where it is not focusable already, or a
            keyboard never reaches the hint.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A deal's actions in a menu the keyboard can walk">
        <Example
            id="menu-deal-actions"
            title={'`<Menu label>` — a button and the actions it opens'}
            summary="A deal moves to another stage from a menu beside it, and the stage it is in is offered but disabled."
            files={{
                'src/deals/lib/DealActions.abide': dealActionsSource,
                'src/deals/lib/DealActions.test.ts': dealActionsTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <DealActions />
        </Example>

        <p><code>Menu</code> renders a button labelled <code>label</code> and a
            <code>role="menu"</code> popover under it. You write the items as its children, each
            an element carrying <code>role="menuitem"</code>, so an item is any button, link or
            form control you already have. Opening focuses the first item. The arrow keys walk
            the items and wrap, Home and End jump to either end, and a disabled item is passed
            over.</p>

        <p>A pick runs the item's own <code>onclick</code>, closes the menu and hands focus back
            to the button, and Escape does the same without a pick. The menu is
            <code>popover="auto"</code>, so a click anywhere else closes it too. The button
            carries <code>aria-haspopup</code> and <code>aria-expanded</code>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`place` — the placement a tooltip and a menu share">
        <p><code>Tooltip</code> and <code>Menu</code> both open where <code>place</code> puts
            them, and an overlay of your own can call it the same way. It reads the anchor's box,
            the floating element's and the viewport's, and hands back where the floating
            element's top-left corner goes, for an element positioned <code>fixed</code>. It writes
            nothing, so a frame placing several elements reads all of them before writing any.
            Import it from <code>@abide/abide</code>.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>side</code></td><td>the side tried first, <code>'bottom'</code> by default; the opposite one is taken where this one has no room and that one has more</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>align</code></td><td><code>'start'</code>, <code>'center'</code> or <code>'end'</code>: the anchor's edge it lines up with</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>offset</code></td><td>pixels between the anchor and the element</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>margin</code></td><td>pixels kept from the viewport's edges; the element is pushed back inside them</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>The <code>Placement</code> it hands back carries <code>x</code>, <code>y</code>, the
            <code>side</code> taken and <code>arrow</code>, how far along the facing edge the
            anchor's middle falls.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Styling tooltips and menus by part and state">
        <p>abide ships no styles for them, and writes one computed style: the <code>inset</code> that
            places the popover, beside an <code>--arrow-offset</code> property for an arrow you
            draw.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>What it is</th><th>Target it with</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>&lt;span role="tooltip"&gt;</code></td><td>the root of <code>Tooltip</code>, the hint itself</td><td><code>class</code> on <code>Tooltip</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>&lt;div&gt;</code></td><td>the root of <code>Menu</code>, around its button and its menu</td><td><code>class</code> on <code>Menu</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-part="trigger"</code></td><td>the menu's button</td><td><code>[&amp;_[data-part=trigger]]:…</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-part="menu"</code></td><td>the <code>role="menu"</code> popover</td><td><code>[&amp;_[data-part=menu]]:…</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-open</code></td><td>on the root while the hint or the menu is open</td><td><code>data-[open]:…</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-side</code></td><td>the side taken, after a flip: on the hint, and on the menu part</td><td><code>data-[side=top]:…</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--arrow-offset</code></td><td>where along the facing edge the anchor's middle falls</td><td><code>before:left-(--arrow-offset)</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <Snippet source={stageHintsSource} path="src/pipeline/lib/StageHints.abide" from={['<Tooltip']} />

        <p>The arrow is a <code>before:</code> square turned 45 degrees, set along the edge by
            <code>--arrow-offset</code> and moved to the edge facing the anchor by
            <code>data-[side=top]:</code>. A popover opens with the browser's own border, padding
            and <code>overflow: auto</code>, so a class resets each one it should not keep.</p>

        <p>A closed popover is hidden by the browser's <code>display: none</code>, and a
            <code>display</code> class of yours beats it, so a closed menu stays on screen. Set
            one behind <code>open:</code>, as the menu card does with
            <code>[&amp;_[data-part=menu]]:open:flex</code>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
